  "text": "Question two. When can you trust an LLM as a judge? Only after you've measured it. Zheng and colleagues documented three biases: position, favoring the answer shown first; verbosity, favoring the longer one; and self-enhancement, favoring text from its own model family. Here's position bias in numbers. A judge prefers version A of the assistant, shown first, over version B in seventy percent of pairs. Rerun every pair with B shown first, and B wins sixty-five percent. The answers didn't change, only their order. Whichever goes first wins about two thirds of the time, and at least thirty-five percent of the verdicts flipped with the order. So score each pair in both orders, and count a win only when the two verdicts agree. Grade against a specific pass-fail rubric, not a score from one to ten. And use a judge from a different model family than the system it grades. Then calibrate it against people. Experts label a sample, and you report the judge's agreement with them, corrected for chance with Cohen's kappa, before you rely on its scores. Change the judge's model or its rubric, and it's a new instrument: calibrate it again. But a judge grades the answer, not whether the right passage was ever found.",
